<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Barbiturate use disorder with physiological dependence (F13.20)</content>. Daily secobarbital 200 to 300 mg for years means a high risk of seizures and delirium if it is stopped or cut quickly. Dr. Grünewald agrees that the change should be made in hospital: a planned four-day admission to the addiction medicine service to change to phenobarbital, a long-acting barbiturate, and find the dose that keeps her free of withdrawal, then a slow outpatient taper. Safety reviewed: no thoughts of suicide or self-harm; the unused capsules are gone and the supply is locked away. [S]<list><item>Admission planned for one week from today.</item><item>Continue secobarbital 200 mg at bedtime until admission, seven days at a time; no alcohol.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Stimulant use disorder (prescribed amphetamine), mild (F15.10)</content>. No extra doses this week. Stopping the stimulant can bring tiredness and low mood for a few weeks, so it will be tapered once phenobarbital is stable, not at the same time as the barbiturate change. [S]<list><item>Continue dextroamphetamine 10 mg each morning until the four-week visit.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Depression with anxious distress (F32.1)</content>. Sertraline 25 mg tolerated for a week. [S]<list><item>Increase sertraline to 50 mg daily.</item><item>Weekly psychotherapy, which she began this week.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Barbiturate dose, adherence, extra doses, and withdrawal signs (tremor, sweating, pulse, sleep, seizure warning signs)</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>200 mg on six nights and 300 mg on one; no withdrawal signs</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Setting for barbiturate withdrawal</td><td>Before any reduction</td><td>Ordered</td><td>Four-day admission to the addiction medicine service planned for next week, as advised today</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Prescription drug monitoring program check</td><td>Before every controlled-substance prescription</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Only this clinic&apos;s prescriptions since intake</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Urine drug testing, explained as part of care</td><td>At the start, after the change to phenobarbital, and periodically during the taper</td><td>Up to date</td><td>At intake: barbiturates and amphetamine only; no opioids, fentanyl or benzodiazepines</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Limited dispensing, locked storage and take-back of unused capsules</td><td>Seven days at first, then two weeks at a time</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Seven-day supply; locked box; 38 capsules and 22 tablets taken back today</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Stimulant dose, extra doses, cravings, and blood pressure and pulse</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>10 mg each morning, no extra doses; blood pressure 124/80, pulse 92</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>CBT for insomnia, sleep diary and Insomnia Severity Index</td><td>Weekly CBT-I sessions; sleep diary and index at each visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>First CBT-I session booked; sleep diary started; Insomnia Severity Index 21 (moderate)</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>New hypnotic (benzodiazepine, Z-drug, trazodone or antihistamine)</td><td>Not recommended as a replacement</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>No new hypnotic during the barbiturate taper</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Depression (PHQ-9)</td><td>At least monthly during acute treatment</td><td>Up to date</td><td>PHQ-9 14 a week ago; next at the four-week visit</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Sertraline adherence and side effects</td><td>Two weeks after starting or changing the dose, then each visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No side effects on 25 mg; increased to 50 mg</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Suicide-risk review (C-SSRS screen since the last visit), safety plan, means safety and the 988 Suicide &amp; Crisis Lifeline</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>C-SSRS screen negative since the last visit; unused capsules returned; the 988 Suicide &amp; Crisis Lifeline in her phone</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Psychotherapy</td><td>Weekly</td><td>Completed today</td><td>First session this week</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Weight, meals and disordered-eating screen, without a weight target</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Weight 46.8 kg; eating one or two meals a day</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Naloxone</td><td>Reassess at every visit and whenever an opioid is prescribed</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>No opioids prescribed or used; naloxone would not reverse a barbiturate</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Addiction medicine consultation</td><td>At the start of treatment</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Seen by Dr. Grünewald today</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>Change from secobarbital to phenobarbital under supervision without a withdrawal seizure or delirium. In progress; target 2026-07-20 [S]</item><item>Lower the phenobarbital dose slowly, at no more than about 20% every two weeks, toward stopping. In progress; target 2026-10-26 [S]</item><item>Stop dextroamphetamine with a short taper and no return to it. In progress; target 2026-08-31 [S]</item><item>Sleep six hours or more on most nights using CBT for insomnia. In progress; target 2026-10-26 [S]</item><item>PHQ-9 below 5 by six months. In progress; target 2026-10-26 [S]</item><item>Regular meals and weight she is comfortable with, without dieting drugs. In progress; target 2026-10-26 [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: Joint Clinical Practice Guideline on Benzodiazepine Tapering: Considerations When Risks Outweigh Benefits (2025); The ASAM Clinical Practice Guideline on Alcohol Withdrawal Management (2020); The ASAM/AAAP Clinical Practice Guideline on the Management of Stimulant Use Disorder (2024); Management of Chronic Insomnia Disorder in Adults: A Clinical Practice Guideline From the American College of Physicians (2016); Prescription Drug Monitoring Programs (PDMPs) (2026); VA/DoD Clinical Practice Guideline for Assessment and Management of Patients at Risk for Suicide (2024); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, depression, bipolar, and mood disorders monograph (2026)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Hospital admission in one week. Four-week review after discharge. Weekly psychotherapy and CBT-I. Next scheduled visit 2026-07-20: four-week review eleven days after a supervised hospital change from secobarbital to phenobarbital: starting the outpatient taper and the stimulant taper. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
